<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heliopolis (ancient Egypt)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iunu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heliopolis, Egyptian Iunu and biblical On, was one of the oldest cities of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, now within north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and the chief cult centre of the sun god Ra and of Atum. Its priesthood developed an influential creation theology. Little survives above ground except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a>, and in 640 Arab armies defeated the Byzantine forces nearby in the Battle of Heliopolis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heliopolis-ancient-egypt.jpg" alt="Heliopolis (ancient Egypt)" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CairoObeliskSesostris1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
